#: dialogs.c:4112
msgid "Audio settings saved"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4131
msgid "Every keyboard shortcut. Change them in Input > Hotkeys."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4145
msgid "Action"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4148
msgid "Shortcut"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4177
msgid "Change any of these under Input > Hotkeys. Player 1 keyboard defaults: arrows or WASD, Num 1 2 3 = A B C, Num 4 5 6 = X Y Z, Enter = Start, Right Shift = Mode (Input > Controls). A connected gamepad works without setup."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "Every menu, and the Settings, Controls, Cheats and Netplay windows, in one place. Keyboard shortcuts are in brackets (the original keys; Input > Hotkeys changes them)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "FILE"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Open Game... (Ctrl+O):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Pick a game file to play: Mega Drive / Genesis (and Sega Pico, which uses the same file types), Master System, Game Gear, SG-1000, Mega CD, 32X (run by PicoDrive: needs picodrive_libretro.dll in the Plugins folder next to the program; some settings of the Genesis Plus GX core do not apply to those), or a .zip / .7z / .gz that holds one."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   A .zip or .7z archive that holds more than one game (several ROMs, or the same game in several regions) opens a window to choose which one to play: type part of a name in the search box, then double-click the game or press Play. The list has Name and Size titles; right-clicking the titles offers Size All Columns to Fit. An archive with one game starts at once, and so does a game kept with its .cue sheet and audio files. The window is also used when the archive is opened from the recent files, the game list or by dragging it onto the window."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Game Folder... (Ctrl+R):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Shows the game browser for a folder of games. Double-click a game to play it, right-click for more choices (play with a saved state, cheats, cover image, clear the recently played list: it is grayed when there is nothing to clear); right-clicking empty space shows the same menu with the choices for a game grayed; with more than one game folder set, Open Game Folder opens a list of them. Folders inside it are searched too (the Scan Subfolders box of the Game Folders window, on by default)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Recent Files:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Your last games, each with its console on the right. If a game's file is gone, the program says so and asks whether to remove it from this list (and from Recently Played); the same question comes from the Recently Played group."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Change Disc...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   A Mega CD command to swap the disc by hand, for games that do not ask for it themselves: pick the next disc's .cue or .chd file. For a game started with Open Disc from Drive a file dialog cannot pick the next disc, so a box asks you to put it into the same drive (use the drive's own eject button) and press OK; the drive is read again (in both cores, also for a Mega CD + 32X game). It also works for a Mega CD + 32X game (run by PicoDrive), with a .cue, .iso or .chd disc image: Fahrenheit (North America) is played that way, with Disc 1 (the Key Disc) opened as a Mega CD 32X disc (the file type Mega CD 32X / Sega CD 32X in File > Open Game, or Ctrl held while opening a disc from a drive), because Disc 1 alone only shows the Sega CD version; when the game has found the 32X it asks for Disc 2, the 32X version: a box asks for the next disc by itself (as it does for the other multi-disc games) and you pick Disc 2's .cue; File > Change Disc... does it by hand too. Tried with Fahrenheit's second disc (File > Change Disc and the automatic box)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Run Mega CD BIOS:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Starts the Mega CD's own BIOS with no disc (USA, Europe or Japan), to reach its CD player and settings screen. It needs bios_CD_U.bin, bios_CD_E.bin or bios_CD_J.bin in the BIOS folder. It is not added to the recent files or the played list."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Open Disc from Drive:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Plays a Sega CD disc straight from an optical drive (a virtual drive such as WinCDEmu counts too), with no image made first. With one Sega CD disc in the drives the entry starts it at once; with two or more it opens a list of them (the drive and the name of the game). The drives are looked at the first time the File menu opens and again when a drive or a disc comes or goes. The data track is read with normal reads. The audio tracks (the CD music) need a drive that gives raw audio sectors; when it does not, the game plays without its music and says so. The Mega CD BIOS is needed as for any Mega CD game. A game started this way is not added to the Recent Files and Recently Played lists; start it again from this menu item."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Mega CD 32X / Sega CD 32X (a file type of File > Open Game):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   For the few games that need a Mega CD and a 32X together (Night Trap, Corpse Killer: Graveyard Edition, Slam City with Scottie Pippen, Supreme Warrior, the Brazilian Surgical Strike 32X CD, and Fahrenheit, which needs two discs: see below). Pick the disc's .cue sheet (or .iso); the disc is started in PicoDrive, which has both machines. The program also looks for such a disc by itself, so Open Disc from Drive and opening a disc image both work for them: when a Mega CD disc is opened, a disc whose header says SEGA 32X is taken at once, and for the others the first 16 MB of its data track are searched for the signs of the 32X (the text \"MARS\" and the header \"SEGA 32X\"; it stops as soon as both are found), which takes a few seconds for a disc in a drive. A disc that has them is started in PicoDrive, and the answer is remembered by the disc's name or file path, so the search is made once. Choosing the file type Mega CD 32X / Sega CD 32X in File > Open Game (below Mega CD / Sega CD) says it by hand for an image (Night Trap and others also exist as plain Mega CD games, and the check can be wrong). Hold Shift while opening a disc to start it as a plain Mega CD disc and keep it that way; hold Ctrl while opening a disc from a drive to start it with the 32X. With Enable Log on, the check and its result are in the log. The Mega CD BIOS for the disc's region is needed, the 32X needs none. PicoDrive here reads .cue/.bin/.iso and .chd discs (a sheet's music as .wav or .ogg, not MP3). The game is handled as a 32X game, and it needs a fast PC. It has been tried with Slam City with Scottie Pippen (from an image and from a disc in a drive) and with the two discs of Fahrenheit (images and a .chd)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   To play Fahrenheit with the 32X (North America; the only game known to need two discs): open Disc 1 (the Key Disc) with the file type Mega CD 32X / Sega CD 32X in File > Open Game (or hold Ctrl while opening it from a drive). The game finds the 32X and asks for Disc 2; the program shows a box asking for the next disc: pick Disc 2's .cue (a .cue with its .bin, or a .chd), then press Start as the game says. To play the plain Sega CD game, just open Disc 1 normally: it never asks for Disc 2. Brutal: Above the Claw is a 32X cartridge game and Tomcat Alley is a Sega CD game only: neither needs this."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Apply Patch...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Pick an .ips or .bps patch file (or a .zip or .7z that holds one) and it is applied in memory to the running game (the item is gray while no game runs), which is then started again from the patched copy: for translations and hacks. A .bps patch made for a different game is refused. The game file itself is not changed."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Game Information...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The game's name, console, genre, whether it is a favorite, file size and location, the CRC32, the cartridge header (console name, type, titles, product code, region, copyright, supported controllers, save RAM), and the ROM size and checksum. Right-clicking the column titles offers Size All Columns to Fit."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Open Genesis Plus EX Folder:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Opens the program folder: settings, saves, states, screenshots, recordings, cheats, covers and BIOS files."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Exit:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Quits."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "EMULATION"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Pause (F2 / Pause), Stop (F3):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Freeze the game, or end it."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Reset (F4), Hard Reset (Ctrl+F4):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Reset is the console's reset button. Hard Reset power-cycles the console (saved game data is kept)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Load State:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Load State from Selected Slot (F8) restores the exact moment saved in the selected slot. Load State from Slot (keys 0-9) loads any of the slots 0-9 directly, whichever is selected (empty slots are grayed). Undo Load State (Ctrl+F8) puts the game back as it was just before the last load; one level, cleared when the game is closed."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Save State:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Save State to Selected Slot (F5) saves the exact moment into the selected slot. Save State to Slot (Shift+0 - Shift+9) saves into any of the slots 0-9 directly. Undo Save State puts the slot back as it was before the last save (or empties it if it was empty); one level, cleared when the game is closed."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Select State Slot:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Choose slot 0-9 for F5 / F8 (F6 / F7 = previous / next). Each entry shows when the slot was last saved."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Load State from File..., Save State to File...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Load a state from, or save the state to, any file you choose (extension .gpstate), for example to send it to a friend or keep it outside the slots."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Manage States...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Thumbnails of all ten slots: left-click loads one, right-click saves to it, saves it to a file (Save This Slot As..., not for an empty slot) or deletes it (not for an empty slot); a double-click on an empty slot saves to it."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Enable Rewind (Settings > Emulation; Backspace, Hold):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Keeps a history of recent play so holding Backspace runs it backwards. Uses 256 MB by default and some CPU while on; switch it off if you never rewind. Not available during netplay."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Rewind History Size (Settings > Emulation):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   How much memory the Rewind history may use: 128, 256 (the default), 512 or 768 MB. More memory lets you go further back (how far depends on the game: busy scenes take more room); changing it starts the history again. The largest sizes may not be available in the 32-bit program."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Rewind Speed (Settings > Emulation):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   How fast Rewind runs backwards: Slow (half the speed), Normal (the default), Fast (twice) or Very Fast (four times). The faster ones go through the history sooner."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Pause When Inactive:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Pauses the game while another window has focus."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Mute When Inactive:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Silences the game while another window has focus; it keeps running (Pause When Inactive stops it). In Settings > General."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Hide Mouse Pointer:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Hides the pointer over a running game in a window and in fullscreen: Never, Always, or after it has been still for 2, 3, 5 or 10 seconds (moving the mouse shows it again). In a Sega Pico game the pointer is always hidden whatever is chosen (the game draws the pen itself) (Settings > Input)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Keep Only One Instance:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Off by default. When on, starting the program again (or opening a game with it) brings the running copy to the front and opens the game there, instead of starting a second copy that would share and overwrite the same settings and saves. Takes effect the next time the program starts; a launch from a frontend (--exit-on-close) is not affected. In Settings > General."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Keep the PC Awake While Playing:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Stops Windows from sleeping, dimming the screen or starting the screen saver while a game is running, and lets it do so again when the game stops. In Settings > General."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Confirm Exit While Playing:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Asks what to do when you close the program while a game is running (on by default): Stop Emulation (back to the game list, the program stays), Exit or Cancel. In Settings > General."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Game Speed (Ctrl++, Ctrl+-, Ctrl+0):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Steps the speed of the game (gray while no game runs) through 25, 50, 75, 100, 125, 150, 200, 300 and 400% and shows it on screen; Ctrl+0 goes back to 100%. The sound follows the speed: below 100% it is stretched and plays lower, above 100% short slices play at the normal pitch (a little choppy). The speed returns to 100% when the game is closed, and it is not available during netplay."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Auto Resume:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Saves the game when you close it (or quit) and, the next time you open that game, asks whether to carry on from there. The question has a \"Do not ask again\" box that remembers your answer; the Restore Dialog button next to Auto Resume in Settings brings it back. It uses a state of its own, so your ten save slots are untouched. On by default; when you close a running game the exit question has a \"Resume this game next time\" box."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Fast Forward (Tab), Rewind (Backspace):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Ticked items that keep the game running fast, or backwards, until you untick them (Rewind needs Enable Rewind in Settings > Emulation). The keys themselves work while held; they can be changed under Input > Hotkeys. While the game is paused, each press of the rewind key goes back one frame (held longer, frame after frame). Only one of Fast Forward and Rewind is on at a time. For slow motion use Game Speed below 100%. How fast Fast Forward runs (2x to 16x, 4x by default) is Fast Forward Speed in Settings > Emulation."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "VIEW"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  View Mode:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   List View or Grid View: how the game browser shows your games."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  List Columns:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   A Sega Pico game (a Mega Drive file whose header says SEGA PICO) is listed as its own console, Sega Pico. Choose which columns the list view shows: Favorite (a star: click it to mark a game as a favorite) and Console (Name is always shown), plus Genre, Region (read from tags such as (USA) in the file name), File Size and File Type (md, bin, chd and so on; for a game in an archive, what is in it and then the archive, such as MD (ZIP) or 32X (7Z)), which are hidden at first and shown after Console. Drag a column title to move the column, and drag the edge between titles to change its width; the order and the widths you leave are remembered. Click a column's title to sort by it; Shift+click more titles to sort by them as well, in that order (for example Console, then Genre, then name); Shift+click one again to flip it, while a plain click starts over (View > Game List > Show Recently Played shows what you played last). Right-clicking the column header offers the same choices, and also Size All Columns to Fit, which gives every shown column the width its contents need. The choices are grayed out while the grid view is shown."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Theme:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   System (follow Windows), Light, Dark, or one of the colored dark themes: Sapphire, Ruby, Emerald, Amethyst, Amber, Aquamarine and Diamond (they tint the grays of the dark theme and set the default accent color; choosing a theme puts the accent color back to Default; pick one in Settings > General afterward if you want another)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Style (Settings > General):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Classic (the look the program has always had) or Modern: flat, rounded buttons, check boxes, radio buttons and drop-down lists (the box and the list that opens), tabs with an accent line under the chosen one, softer toolbar buttons, the chosen game or row in the accent color, rounded window corners and a title bar and border in the color of the theme (Windows 11), and Segoe UI text, also in the menus (changed at once in the windows that are open, both ways). It is also in View > Style, and goes with Theme: any Style can be Light or Dark."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Interface Size (Settings > General):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Normal or Large menus and dialogs. Changing it restarts the program. A window that is too big for the screen (a small screen, Large size or a high Windows scale such as 175%) gets scroll bars instead of cutting off its controls."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Hide All Bars (View > Toolbars, Ctrl+H):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   While a game runs, takes the menu bar, the toolbar and the status bar away, so the window shows the game only (for an arcade cabinet or a stream); in fullscreen it also stops the menu bar and the toolbar from coming in when the mouse touches the top edge. The same key brings them back; it is not saved, and the game list keeps its bars."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Show Toolbar Labels (View > Toolbars):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Writes the name of each toolbar button (Open, Play, Settings ...) under its icon; switch it off for icons only."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Show Toolbar, Show Search Bar (Ctrl+F), Show Filter Bar:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Switch the rows above the game list on or off. The toolbar has buttons for open game, refresh, favorites only, search (the same as Ctrl+F), play, pause, stop, reset, switch fullscreen, screenshot, settings, the Video and Audio settings tabs and the player controls; a star button next to Refresh shows only your favorites. The toolbar stays above the game while it runs in a window; in fullscreen it slides in at the top edge of the screen together with the menu bar (the picture makes room for it; View > Toolbars > Toolbar in Fullscreen switches that on, and it is off by default). The search bar narrows the list as you type (Ctrl+F jumps to it; Close or Show Search Bar hides it, and Show Search Bar brings it back). Search words narrow by one field: region:usa, console:mega cd, genre:action, type:chd, fav:yes; anything else you type is looked for in the name (for example sonic region:usa). The filter bar narrows it by console, genre (looked up from the game's title in a table built into the program; a game that is not in it is listed as Unknown) and region (read from tags such as (USA) in the file name). Hiding the filter bar clears its choices, so the list shows every game again. Show Status Bar (View > Toolbars, on by default) shows or hides the bar along the bottom. Toolbar Icon Size (Small, Normal, the default, or Large; also in View > Toolbars > Toolbar Size) and Show Toolbar Labels (the name of each button under its icon, on by default) are in Settings > General. When the window is too narrow for all the buttons, the ones that do not fit go on a second row (a third if needed), like a menu bar, and the bar grows taller; they return to one row when the window is wide enough again."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Toolbar in Fullscreen:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   In fullscreen, shows the toolbar together with the menu bar when the mouse touches the top edge of the screen."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Show Favorites Only:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Mark a game as a favorite by clicking its star in the Favorite column (the right-click menu has Add to Favorites too). Show Favorites Only, or the star on the toolbar, hides every other game. Sorting by the Favorite column puts your favorites first."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Show Recently Played:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Shows the last few games you opened as a group at the top of the list, above all the others (they are in the full list too). On by default; choose it again to switch it off."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Clean Game Names:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The game list shows \"Sonic The Hedgehog\" instead of \"Sonic The Hedgehog (USA, Europe) [!].md\". Only the list (and File > Recent Files) changes, not the files. On by default."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Show Grid Lines:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Thin horizontal lines between the rows of the lists; the lines between columns are always shown. On by default."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Group by Console:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Splits the list into sections, one per console (Genesis, Master System, Mega CD ...), each with a heading row in the same style as Recently Played. The grid view has the same headings (Recently Played, All Games, Favorites and one per console): each starts a new row of covers. The empty space above the first heading shows the Genesis Plus EX logo."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Show Letter Strip:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The Letter Strip is a column of letters beside the list: click a letter, or drag along it, to jump to the games starting with it (the letter under the pointer grows) (# is for names starting with a digit or a symbol); letters with no game are dimmed."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  List Spacing:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Compact, Normal or Expanded spacing between the rows of the game list (grayed while a game is running)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Always on Top:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Keeps the window above every other window (also in Settings > General)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Reset Window Size:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Puts the window back at the size chosen under Video > Window Size (a size you dragged the window to is forgotten), in the middle of the screen."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "VIDEO"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Window Size:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   1x to 10x (3x by default; a size larger than the screen is brought down to fit when the program starts)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Fullscreen (Alt+Enter), Start Game in Fullscreen:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Esc also toggles fullscreen. In fullscreen, move the mouse to the top edge of the screen to show the menu bar; it hides again when the mouse moves away. The mouse cursor stays visible in fullscreen and hides itself after a few idle seconds. Closing the game leaves fullscreen and returns to the selected window size. Fullscreen Monitor (below) says which monitor it uses."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Render Filter:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Pixel-art scalers and CRT looks (Scale2x, Scale4x, MMPX, MMPX Enhanced, 4xSaI, xBR, HQ2x, xBRZ, Scanlines, CRT...) applied before the picture is shown, at a fixed multiple. The resizing methods (Bilinear, Sharp Bilinear, Bicubic ...) are in Scaling Filter, which resizes the picture to the window."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Scaling Filter:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   How the picture is resized to the window, after the Render Filter. Nearest: sharp pixels; Sharp Nearest: the picture is enlarged by a whole multiple with sharp pixels first and smoothed only for the rest, so pixels stay crisp and even, without the borders of Integer Scaling (it looks close to Nearest); Bilinear: soft edges; Sharp Bilinear: Bilinear with a narrow blend (about half a source pixel, on a smooth curve), so much less blur; Bicubic: smoother than Bilinear with a little more edge contrast. Sharp Bilinear and Bicubic are pixel shaders with Direct3D 9, 11 and 12; the classic renderer does Sharp Bilinear on the processor and has no Bicubic (grayed)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Shaders:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Runs a librashader (.slangp) shader chain on top of the picture, with the Direct3D 11 or Direct3D 12 renderer, and 64-bit exe only (the 32-bit exe has no Shaders menu); librashader.dll ships in the Plugins folder next to the exe (see the README). Preset Browser lists the presets of the Shaders folder (every folder below it) in one window: type in the Search box to narrow the list (every word has to be in the name), and the preset you pick is used on the running game at once and kept (it is marked \"(in use)\" in the list); OK closes the window, Cancel puts back the preset that was in use when it opened. None switches shaders off, Parameters opens the preset's own settings and Open Folder opens the Shaders folder. A preset that does not load says why under the list."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Visual Effects:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Integer Scaling (enlarges the picture by whole multiples only, 1x, 2x, 3x ..., so every pixel is the same size; it is centered, with borders, when the window is between two sizes, and follows the Aspect Ratio setting for the shape of the pixels), Brighten (a gentle brightness lift), Deinterlacing (for a game in the 448-line interlaced mode, such as Sonic the Hedgehog 2 in 2P Vs.: both fields of the picture are drawn from the same moment in every frame, so nothing that moves is combed; on at first; Interlaced Mode is not used while it is on), LCD Ghosting (bright pixels fade slowly, like a handheld screen), Color Correction (Light or Full: the paler, duller colors of a handheld LCD screen, with the colors bleeding into each other a little, for every console; the Picture Adjustments still apply on top)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Picture Adjustments...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   A window with Brightness, Contrast, Saturation, Gamma and Hue, each a slider that acts while you drag it (Restore Defaults puts them back to normal, Cancel puts back what they were when the window opened, OK keeps them), alongside Brighten (Settings > Video; the window is also a button there). They work with every renderer."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Flicker Blending, Dither Smoothing:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Flicker Blending joins the two frames of an object that a game shows every other frame to fake transparency, so it looks solid. Dither Smoothing softens the checkerboard patterns many games use to fake extra colors (Settings > Video)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Bezel Image...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Draws an image (a PNG whose middle is see-through: a TV, an arcade cabinet) over the picture as a frame; Remove Bezel goes back to plain bars. The picker starts in the bezels folder next to the program (made for you). The bezel is drawn in fullscreen only (Settings > Video, in the Bezel and Border frame)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Shader Parameters...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Adjusts the loaded shader preset's own parameters while it runs: the settings are in a list with a Search box (a setting that has no description is shown by its own name in brackets), and the picked one has a slider (a change takes effect at once while you move it; OK keeps the changes, Cancel puts the settings back as they were when the window opened) that jumps from one value to the next (1, 2, 3 ...) as the preset defines them, without editing its file; the values are remembered for that preset (Video > Shaders, and Settings > Video in the shader box under the preset file; grayed until a shader that has settings is loaded)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Skip Render Filter with Shaders:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   While a shader runs, the Render Filter is not applied first, so the shader gets the game's own picture instead of one the filter already enlarged. Shaders that scale by whole multiples or place a bezel work out their sizes from the picture they receive, so one that looks too small or wrong with a Render Filter on may look right with this ticked. Off by default; has no effect without a shader or a Render Filter (Video > Shaders, below Shader Parameters, and Settings > Video, in the shader box beside it; Direct3D 11 and 12)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Border Color:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The color of the bars around the picture when the window or screen is a different shape: black, dark gray, gray, white, navy, dark green or dark red (Settings > Video)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Fullscreen Mode:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Borderless Window, Exclusive Fullscreen or True Exclusive Fullscreen (Settings > Video). Exclusive Fullscreen switches the screen to the Fullscreen Resolution for the game and puts it back afterwards."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   True Exclusive Fullscreen: needs the Direct3D 11 or Direct3D 12 renderer (another setup uses Exclusive Fullscreen instead); Variable Refresh Rate is grayed and off while it is chosen. The game takes the chosen monitor for itself (real exclusive fullscreen on a window of its own) at the screen's own resolution (the Fullscreen Resolution choice is not used with it), so Windows no longer composes the picture, which can even out the frames when a second monitor runs at another refresh rate. The menu and toolbar cannot be reached then; Alt+Enter or Esc leaves it, and so does anything that takes the focus (Alt+Tab, a dialog)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Fullscreen Monitor:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Which monitor the game goes fullscreen on (Current Monitor, or one of your monitors). Used by Borderless Window, Exclusive Fullscreen and True Exclusive Fullscreen; it applies the next time you go fullscreen (Settings > Video)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Fullscreen Resolution:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The screen size used when Fullscreen Mode is Exclusive Fullscreen: a drop-down of the sizes your monitor offers (Desktop Resolution changes nothing). Under Window Size in Settings > Video."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Scanlines:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Dark lines between rows: Off, 25, 50, 75 or 100%."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  NTSC Filter:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Composite, S-Video or RGB television look. For a 32X game it is the program's own version of the look (the picture keeps its size), not the Genesis Plus GX core's."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Rotation:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Turns the picture by 90 degrees clockwise, 180 degrees or 90 degrees counterclockwise, for games and demos made to be played with the screen on its side (also under Video, in Settings). Only what is on the screen is turned: screenshots, recordings and light-gun aiming are not."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Aspect Ratio:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Original Console Pixels (the default: the Mega Drive's own pixels, 8:7 wide in the 256-pixel mode and 32:35 in the 320-pixel one, which makes the picture about 1.31 times as wide as it is high; other consoles use square pixels), 1:1 (Square Pixels), 4:3 (Standard, as on a CRT television) or 16:9 (Widescreen: stretches the picture to fill a widescreen window)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Limit Redraws:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Draws the picture at most 30, 40, 50 or 60 times a second while the game itself still runs at full speed. Helps a weak PC or a very fast monitor; Off draws every frame (Settings > Video)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Borders:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Hides or shows the overscan borders (top/bottom, left/right)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Interlaced Mode (Settings > Video):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Not used while Deinterlacing is on. Single Field or Double Field, for games that use the 448-line interlaced mode. Single Field shows the same half of the picture every frame, so it stays steady. Double Field shows both fields at once; where something moves, the older field's lines are rebuilt from the new ones (the new lines stay exactly as the game drew them), so there are hardly any comb teeth and a moving object stays sharp (a pattern that repeats and scrolls, such as blades of grass, is found too); a still picture keeps both fields. With Original Console Pixels or Square Pixels the picture is drawn at the shape of the 224-line one, not tall."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Frameskip:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Skips drawing some frames on a slow PC (Off, Auto, or Manual by audio-buffer level). Not used during netplay or recording."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  VSync:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Waits for the screen's refresh after each frame so motion is even. Works with any renderer (Direct3D 11 and 12 wait in their swap chain, the others wait for the Windows compositor); it is skipped for a frame whenever the sound buffer runs low, so it never causes crackle. It is not used (grayed out) while Variable Refresh Rate is on. Off by default."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Smooth Motion:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The console runs at about 49.70 frames a second (PAL) or 59.92 (NTSC), a screen at 50 or 60 Hz: with VSync on, one picture in every few seconds is shown twice, which shows as a small stutter in steady scrolling (a PAL game on a 50 Hz screen shows it too). With this on (off by default), when the screen's real refresh rate is within 2% of the game's own, the game runs at that rate, so every picture is shown once. The speed and the pitch change by less than 1%. With Fullscreen Mode set to Exclusive Fullscreen it also switches the screen to 50 Hz for a PAL game and to 60 Hz for an NTSC game while the game is fullscreen, in the size it has, and puts it back when you leave fullscreen or close the game (only the monitor that shows the game is changed; a screen without such a mode stays as it is, and the log says so). A PAL game on a 60 Hz screen cannot be matched (50 against 60); the speed matching is only used with VSync on, and not with Variable Refresh Rate, in netplay, while recording, in Mega CD and 32X games, or at a Game Speed other than 100%. With Enable Log on, the log says when it is used."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Use Alternate Timing:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Normally the sound card sets the pace of the game. With this on, the PC's precise clock does (the last millisecond before a frame is waited for by looking at the clock, not by sleeping), and the sound follows: the pace is nudged by up to half a percent a frame to keep the sound queue between two and three frames. The frames then start at even times, which can make steady scrolling smoother on some PCs, mostly with a PAL game; the price is that a very rare click or skip of the sound can happen. It is not used in netplay or at a Game Speed other than 100%, and it does nothing with the sound off (the clock paces then anyway). Off by default."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Frame Timing in the Log:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   With Enable Log on, the log has Frame timing lines for a game: how far apart the frames came (shortest, longest and average, in milliseconds), how many were slow, how long showing a frame took, and the real refresh rate of the screen the game is on; a summary for the whole game is written when it closes. The Display lines at the start name each monitor with its exact refresh rate. Send the log when scrolling or motion is uneven."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Variable Refresh Rate:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   For a display with variable refresh (G-Sync, FreeSync, Adaptive-Sync): the Direct3D 11 or 12 swap chain is made so that each frame is shown the moment it is ready and the display follows the game's own rate (about 59.92 frames a second for an NTSC game, 49.7 for PAL), with no tearing and no frame shown twice or skipped. It needs Windows 10 (version 1607 or later), a graphics driver that supports it and variable refresh turned on in Windows or the driver (in a window the driver may have a separate setting for windowed games). While it is in use it takes the place of VSync, which is then grayed out (it keeps its tick). If it is not available the program says so and goes on as before. Only for the Direct3D 11 and 12 renderers; off by default (Video menu)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Renderer:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   GDI draws with the CPU; Direct3D 9, Direct3D 11 and Direct3D 12 use the graphics card instead (each falls back to GDI automatically if it isn't available; Direct3D 12 needs Windows 10). Direct3D 11 and 12 are what a shader tool such as librashader needs, and they have Variable Refresh Rate and True Exclusive Fullscreen. Direct3D 12 draws the same picture as Direct3D 11: the same Scaling Filters, overlays and shader presets, with a frame latency of one frame."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Adapter:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Which graphics card the Direct3D 9, 11 and 12 renderers use (Settings > Video). Auto lets Windows pick; the other choices are the cards Windows lists, without the software adapter. Changing it makes the renderer again. True Exclusive Fullscreen only works on a monitor of the chosen card (Exclusive Fullscreen is used otherwise). If the saved card is gone, Auto is used."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "AUDIO"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Mute (Ctrl+M):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Silences the sound; untick to hear it again."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Mute DAC Sound (Ctrl+L), DAC Volume:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The DAC is the sample channel of the Genesis FM chip: the drums, voices and effects a game plays as recorded sound (the SEGA shout, the drum hits of many songs). Mute DAC Sound (Audio > Mute) silences it and the DAC Volume slider of Levels and Latency sets its level, from 0% to 150% (100% is as the game plays it). It works for Genesis, Mega CD and Pico games; not for the Master System or Game Gear, which have no DAC, nor for 32X games, which run in PicoDrive. Above 100% the loudest samples can clip, and a low level makes the sound a little coarse (the samples are 8 bits)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Mute FM Sound (Ctrl+N), Mute PSG Sound (Ctrl+B), Mute PWM Sound (Ctrl+K):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Silence the FM chip (the main music), the PSG chip (the square-wave sound) or, in a 32X game, the PWM sound (most of its music and effects) on its own, without touching the saved levels. The level of the PWM sound is the PWM Preamp slider of Levels and Latency."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Mute CD Audio:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Silences the music of the disc of a Mega CD game on its own (the game's other sounds stay). It is grayed while a game that is not a Mega CD game runs: a cartridge, a 32X game, or a game whose music is on disc images beside it (MD+ and MSU-MD). Its key is Ctrl+J (it can be changed in Controls)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Levels and Latency...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Master volume, FM and PSG levels (they work for 32X games too), CD audio (not for 32X), how many frames of sound are buffered, and on the right the audio filter (Off, Low-Pass or a 3-Band EQ) with its settings and the Sega CD PCM volume. The sliders act while you drag them (Cancel puts the old levels back), and a live Level meter at the bottom (left and right) shows what is playing (it stays empty when no game is running); it moves while the game keeps running behind the window."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  High-Quality FM Synthesis, Master System FM Unit and Chip (Settings > Audio):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Band-limited FM, whether the Master System's FM unit is used (auto, off, on), and which chip emulates it."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  FM Chip:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Which Genesis FM sound chip is emulated: YM2612 or YM3438, the exact Nuked versions (default is YM2612 Nuked) or MAME. A 32X game (PicoDrive) has only the two chip types: the YM3438 kinds play as a YM3438, the others as a YM2612."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Audio Backend:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Which Windows sound system plays the game's sound: Windows Classic, XAudio2, WASAPI (Shared; the default) or WASAPI (Exclusive). The newer ones can have lower delay and cope better when the PC is busy. Exclusive gives the game the sound device for itself, for the lowest delay: no other program can play sound on that device while the game runs, and the device has to accept 16-bit stereo at the chosen Sample Rate. If one cannot be opened the game falls back (Exclusive to Shared, then to Windows Classic). Sound Device applies to all of them (Settings > Audio). Takes effect at once."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Console Model:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   A sound preset in the Audio menu (Console Model, called Console Model (Audio) in the Hotkeys list, with the shortcuts Ctrl+Shift+3, 1 and 2): Default puts the program's own sound set-up back (YM2612 Nuked, no low-pass filter); Model 1 is the early, duller console (the YM2612 chip with its low-pass filter), Model 2 the later, cleaner one (the YM3438 chip, no filter). They set FM Chip and Low-Pass Filter; changing those yourself leaves none ticked."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Sample Rate:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   22050 Hz (lighter and duller, for a slow PC), 44100 or 48000 Hz."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  3-Band EQ (Settings > Audio, Tone):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Switches the equalizer on or off like the Low-Pass Filter tick (only one of the two can be on); its gains and crossover frequencies are in Levels and Latency."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  High-Quality PSG Resampling, Low-Pass Filter, 3-Band EQ, Mono Output (Audio > Tone and Effects, which also holds Stereo Width, Bass Boost, Overdrive and Even Out Loudness):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Cleaner PSG sound, a soft high-frequency roll-off, and mono instead of stereo."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Stereo Width:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Narrow (50%), Normal, Wide (150%) or Extra Wide (200%): pulls the left and right sound toward the middle or spreads them apart (Settings > Audio)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Bass Boost:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Off, Light, Medium or Strong: lifts the low notes so the music is fuller on laptop speakers and small headphones (Settings > Audio)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Overdrive:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Pushes the sound into a soft limiter: louder, fuller and a little gritty, like a console turned up. Off by default (Settings > Audio)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Even Out Loudness:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Lowers very loud games and lifts quiet ones so they sit at a similar level, without clipping. Off by default (Settings > Audio)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Sound Device...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   A drop-down of your sound devices: which speakers or headphones the game plays through; Windows Default follows the Windows setting (Settings > Audio)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "INPUT"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Controls...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   One window with a tab for Player 1, one for Player 2 and an Extras tab (what is plugged into the console's two controller ports, and the combos). The pad tabs lay the buttons out like a Mega Drive pad in titled frames (D-Pad with its deadzone, System, Buttons with their Turbo ticks) around a picture of the pad. Click a button, then press the key or gamepad button you want (Esc or a right click cancels the wait; right-click a button to set or clear it; clicking its spot on the picture works too). Tick Turbo beside A, B, C, X, Y or Z: held down, the button then fires repeatedly (Turbo Rate sets how fast). A pad button can have a second key or gamepad button (Shift+click it, or right-click it and choose Set Second Key or Set Second Button): either one presses it, and Clear removes both. A key or button can only be on one place: assigning it takes it off the one that had it (also on the other player, for keys), and a line under the pad says what was removed, and when the key is also a shortcut or the pad button also a gamepad hotkey. Auto Assign goes through them all in turn. Pressing a mapped key or gamepad button lights its spot on the picture, so you can check a pad; tick Test Keys to try keys such as Enter, Tab and Esc without them operating the window. You can also set the gamepad thumbstick's deadzone (how far it has to move off-center before it registers at all). The right stick counts as four more buttons (RS Up, Down, Left, Right) that can be assigned like any other; this works for XInput pads and, where the pad reports the stick on its Rx / Ry or Z / Rz axes, for DirectInput ones. Gamepad Hotkeys... opens a window to choose which pad button runs each shortcut, pressed on its own (no Back needed): Fast Forward and Rewind (held), Toggle Turbo, Save State and Load State (current slot), and Previous and Next Slot. By default the right stick does the last four (up = save, down = load, left = previous slot, right = next slot) and LB / LT are Fast Forward / Rewind; the right stick button (RSB) switches Turbo on or off (the buttons keep their Turbo setting). A button used there also reaches the game, so the right stick is a good choice. Gamepad hotkeys use the Xbox-style (XInput) pad of Player 1 only. Player 1 defaults: arrows (WASD as the second key) for the D-pad, Num 1 Num 2 Num 3 = A B C, Num 4 Num 5 Num 6 = X Y Z, Enter = Start, Right Shift = Mode. With the 2-Button Pad chosen as the Controller Model, buttons 1 and 2 take Num 1 and Num 2 (while the keys are still the defaults; choosing another pad puts them back). Restore Defaults (bottom left) opens a list: This Tab (the one that is showing: Player 1, Player 2, or Extras with the combos removed and both ports back to a Control Pad) or All Tabs; only those go back to their defaults (a player's also resets the Turbo Rate)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Hotkeys...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Every keyboard shortcut in one list: double-click an action (or select it and press F2) and press the key you want, alone or with Ctrl, Shift or Alt. A key can only be on one action: the old one loses it. Right-click an action for Restore Default (that one only), Clear (takes its shortcut off) and Clear All (takes every shortcut off); Restore Defaults at the bottom brings every original key back. The menu labels and the Keyboard Shortcuts window follow your choices. Fast Forward and Rewind (the keys you hold, Tab and Backspace by default) are in the list too; their gamepad buttons are under Controls > Gamepad Hotkeys. The Disable Hotkeys box above the list (off by default) turns every keyboard shortcut off at once, the keys of the list included (Fast Forward, Rewind, Advance One Frame): the list is kept, and the menus show no shortcut keys while it is ticked. Leave Fullscreen (Esc) keeps working, so a fullscreen window can still be left. Gamepad hotkeys are not affected. In every list with column titles (this one, Gamepad Hotkeys, Keyboard Shortcuts, Convert Codes, RAM Search, RAM Watch, Choose a Game, and the game, cheat and game information lists), right-clicking the titles offers Size All Columns to Fit."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Pause Menu (Ctrl+P, or a gamepad button chosen under Controls > Gamepad Hotkeys):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   While a game runs: pauses it and shows a small menu in the middle of the picture: Resume, Save State, Load State (the current slot), Save Screenshot, Sound On / Off, Fullscreen, Reset and Quit. Move with the arrow keys or the D-pad up and down, choose with Enter or the pad's A button, and leave with Esc or B (the game goes back to how it was before: paused or running). Not available in netplay."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Sega Pico (Page Down and Page Up):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The voices of a Sega Pico game play (the program makes the console's speech chip), and a save state keeps the voice that is playing."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   D-pad: Player 1's D-pad (the arrow keys or W, A, S, D), or the D-pad or left stick of a gamepad."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Red button: Player 1's A, B or C button (Num 1, Num 2 or Num 3), or the right mouse button."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Pen: the mouse (left button touches). Over the game's picture it is the pen on the pad, where the game draws its own pointer (a hammer, a brush); over the Storyware window it is the pen on the book's page. A gamepad's right stick moves the pen from where it is, and RT or R2, a press of the stick, or Start touches with it."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Previous and Next Page: Page Down and Page Up, or LB and RB of an Xbox pad (L1 and R1 of a PlayStation pad, L and R of a Switch pad: the same places on any pad). The pages go up to 7."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The mouse pointer is always hidden over the game, which draws the pen itself (Hide Mouse Pointer is as chosen for the other games). The gamepad hotkeys on the right stick, Fast Forward and Rewind do not run in a Sega Pico game."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Storyware window: opens with the page keys, and when the game starts (with the closed book). It is as big as the picture of the open page, and what is printed in the book can be clicked with the mouse, as on the real console. The pictures go in Images\\Pico-Storyware\\<game>\\, a folder named like the game's file, each named like the game with the page number after it: <game>_1.png, <game>_2.png ... (_0 for the closed book; PNG, JPG or BMP). They are your own scans; none comes with the program."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Port A / Port B Device:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   What is plugged in: Control Pad, Mouse, a light gun (Light Phaser, Menacer or Justifier, depending on the port), XE-1AP analog joystick, Activator, Paddle Control, Sports Pad, Graphic Board (Pico's drawing tablet), a 4-player Team Player adapter, EA 4-Way Play (4 players; it takes both ports), a Master Tap (the Master System's 4-player adapter) or the Smash Controller; not every device is on both ports. Found in Input > Controls, on the Extras tab (no longer in the Input menu or Settings)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Enable Background Input:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Keeps reading the controller while another window has focus."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Show Light Gun Crosshair:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Shows a crosshair instead of the arrow over the picture while a light gun is plugged in (on by default; Settings > Input only, not in the Input menu)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Invert Mouse Y Axis:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Flips the up/down movement of the Sega Mouse (Settings > Input only, not in the Input menu)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Combo Buttons...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Makes one key or pad button press several game buttons together. It is on the Extras tab of Controls (Input > Controls...), in one Combos frame. The Saved Combos list always shows 12 numbered rows (number, trigger, actions). Click a row (an empty one for a new combo), then click the Trigger button and press the key or gamepad button to use (a letter or digit, F1-F12, Num0-Num9, Space, an arrow key, or a pad button: A B X Y LB RB LT RT Back Start or the D-pad on XInput pads). Then click the game buttons you want in the Actions grid (Up Down Left Right, Start Mode, X Y Z, A B C): each click adds that button to the row, for example Gamepad Y -> Down+A. The Hold button opens a menu that makes the action you just added Held: after the trigger is pressed it stays down by itself, for the time chosen (0.25 to 10 seconds) or until you press that button yourself, shown as Down(Hold 1.0s); Off (No Hold) is the default, the action is then only pressed while the trigger is. The Pause button opens a menu to wait before the next action starts (Pause 0.5s); without one, the action after a Held one starts 70 ms later (Down, then A). Remove Last takes the last action off the row, Delete Combo empties the selected row (up to 12 combos). If every action is Held until you let go, the trigger works as a switch: press it to hold them, press it again to let go. Held actions are also let go when the window loses focus or a game is opened or closed. The How It Works button, at the right end of the list's title row, explains all this with an example. Saved with Controls. They work for Player 1."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Profile (Controls window):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   In the Controls window, a Profile box with Load, Save and Delete: a profile stores both players' keys and buttons, pad types, deadzones, the turbo rate and the combos (not the ports; in the profiles folder next to the program). The Profile box is a drop-down you can also type in: pick a saved profile or type a name, then use Load (loads it into the window, and OK keeps it), New (a new profile from the window, under the name typed in the box), Save (saves the window into the loaded profile; with another name typed in the box it renames the loaded profile, after asking) or Delete. Restore Defaults (next to OK) puts both players' keys, buttons, deadzones, turbo rate and combos back to their defaults; Load and Delete are grayed while no profile is saved."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Mouse Speed:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   How fast the Sega Mouse moves: 50 to 200%."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Pause When the Pad Disconnects:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Pauses the game with a message when Player 1's gamepad is unplugged or its battery runs out, instead of running on without input. Off by default (Settings > Input)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Gun Aim Offset X / Y:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Shifts where the light gun hits by up to 12 pixels, if shots land beside or above where you point."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Light Gun Border:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   While a light gun is plugged in, draws a white frame around the picture and makes the picture smaller to leave room for it: Off, Thin (2%), Medium (4%), Thick (6%) or Extra Thick (8%) of the height of the window. A gun that finds the screen with a camera, such as the Sinden Lightgun, needs it. A gun that works through IR lights (Gun4IR) does not. The aim stays right: it is measured on the picture inside the frame. Off by default (Settings > Input only, not in a menu)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Shooting off the screen:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   With a light gun plugged in, a shot with the pointer on the black bars or the frame around the picture (inside the window) is a shot off the screen, which some games use to reload (Lethal Enforcers, for example). The mouse buttons are the triggers: the left button is the gun's A, the right button B and the middle button C."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Konami Justifiers:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   With Port B Device set to Justifier (Lethal Enforcers, Lethal Enforcers II): the first gun is Player 1's, the second Player 2's. The mouse is the first gun: the left button is its trigger, the right button its Start button, and the Start of Player 1 (key or controller) is its Start too. The second gun has the trigger and the Start of Player 2's own keys or controller, and aims with the same pointer."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Confine Mouse to the Picture:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   While a Sega Mouse or light gun game runs, keeps the pointer inside the picture. Pausing the game, opening a menu or dialog, pressing the Windows key or Alt+Tab lets it go; after the Windows key or Alt+Tab a click in the picture holds it again."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Gamepad Hotkeys:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Under Controls, besides fast forward, rewind and the states, these can each be put on a gamepad button (none by default): Pause, Reset, Screenshot, Fullscreen, Mute, Record Video + Audio, Game Speed Faster and Slower, and the Pause Menu. A box in the same window makes them need a held Hold Button (the last row), so the pad's buttons are free in the game when it is not held. The Pause Menu pauses the game and shows a small menu over the picture: Resume, Save State, Load State, Save Screenshot, Sound On / Off, Fullscreen, Reset and Quit. Move with the D-pad (or the arrow keys), choose with the pad's A or Start (or Enter), and go back with B (or Esc)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "TOOLS"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Cheats... (Ctrl+C):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   With no game running, this opens the cheats of the game picked in the list, without starting it. Add Game Genie, Action Replay or raw codes (also a work RAM byte, aaaaaa:dd), or load a .cht file. Click #, Code, Description or Status to sort the list; the # is the number a cheat got when it was added and stays with it. Right-clicking the column titles offers Size All Columns to Fit. The Status column says Active, Off, Cheats are off, Game not running or Code not valid for this system. ROM codes wait for the first button press after a game starts or is reset (at least two seconds, at most twenty), because a game checks its ROM first. In netplay both PCs need the same cheats on. Convert... shows a code as Game Genie, Action Replay or raw. When other files in the game's console folder under Cheats have a name starting like the game's (\"Sonic The Hedgehog (Action Replay).cht\" beside \"Sonic The Hedgehog (USA).cht\"), the Similar Cheat Files drop-down appears under the buttons to load their cheats (when the list already has cheats it offers Load, which replaces the list, or Insert, which merges the file's cheats into it); it is not shown otherwise. A file in Cheats\\library named after the game's CRC32 (12345678.cht) is loaded by itself for a game that has no cheats of its own."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Cheat Search...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Finds where a value (lives, health, ammo; or with Size 2 bytes a score or money, saved as two cheats; a value above 255 typed for a 1-byte search offers to search it as 2 bytes) is kept in memory, so you can make a cheat without a code (a Mega CD game can also search the CD's own memory, Word RAM in either of its modes: pick it under Memory). A reset or a state load starts the search again; closing the window keeps it until the game stops. Working it through: First Scan, change the value in the game, choose what to Find (Equal to Value, Changed, Unchanged, Increased or Decreased) and click Next Scan (Undo Scan takes the last one back), and repeat until a few addresses are left; select one, type the value to hold in Set to and click Add Cheat. It becomes an ordinary RAM cheat in the Cheats list. A double click on a row opens Change Value, which puts a number into that address once; Freeze holds the Set to value there, each frame, so you can try it at once (the Frozen column shows it; click Unfreeze, or Unfreeze All, to let go; frozen addresses end when the game stops). The window opens beside the main window, can be made bigger, and gets scroll bars when it is too small for what it holds."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Tools > Fix Checksum...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   For a Mega Drive / Genesis game (a hack, a translation, a patched game) whose header checksum ($18E) no longer matches its data. It saves a copy of the game with the checksum made right (the sum of the words from $200 to the ROM End of the header); the game that is open is not changed. If the ROM End in the header is not the end of the game, it asks whether to set it right too. Game Information shows both checksums."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Tools > Debugger... (the Debugger window):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The debug tools share one window, as in gpgx_debugger: the CPU Debugger on top (for a game with a 68000) and, on tabs under it, the Memory Viewer, VDP Viewer, Sound Viewer, CPU Viewer, RAM Search and RAM Watch. The View button at the top left shows a part directly: Memory, any view of the VDP (Registers, Palette, Tiles, Sprites, Plane A, Plane B, Window, Sprite Layer, Sprite Preview, H Scroll, V Scroll), the Sound Viewer or the CPU Viewer, RAM Search and RAM Watch. The window stays open while the game runs and closes when the game stops; drag the bar under the CPU Debugger to resize it; it has its own taskbar button, so it can be minimized; Esc closes it."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Debugger > Memory:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   A window of its own that stays open while the game runs and shows its memory in hex: 68000 RAM, Z80 RAM, VRAM, CRAM, VSRAM and the ROM (a Mega CD game also PRG-RAM and Word RAM, a 32X game its SDRAM and video memories). Pick the memory at the top; type an address and press Enter to jump to it. Bytes that just changed are red. Click a byte and type hex digits to change it (any memory; the video memories as the video chip would write them; not during netplay; a 32X game needs the plugin of the same version as the program). Drag (or hold Shift) to select several bytes; right-click for Copy, Copy Numbers, Copy Characters, Copy Address, Paste, Paste to Numbers, Paste to Characters, Dump to File... (the selection, or the whole memory) and Go To...."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Debugger > VDP, Sound, CPU:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Windows of the same kind (they stay open and update while the game runs, and are read only) for what the chips hold. VDP Viewer (Mega Drive games): choose Registers (pick a register and click Edit Register..., or double-click it, to set its bits), Palette, Tiles (with a palette line), Sprites (in link or table order), Plane A, Plane B, Window (point at a tile for its details, click to lock it), Sprite Layer, Sprite Preview, H Scroll or V Scroll (the scroll of every line or column as a curve); pictures can be zoomed 1x to 8x (Ctrl and the wheel), scrolled and panned with the middle button. Sound Viewer: the FM chip's channels (keys, frequency, algorithm, each operator), the PSG's tones, volumes and noise, or (32X games) the PWM chip's registers, sample rate and last samples. CPU Viewer: the 68000 or Z80 registers (and, for a 32X game, the two SH-2 processors). They work with 32X games too, which needs the picodrive_libretro files of the same version as the program. Buttons: Palette view - Dump Pal and Load Pal (the 64 colors, 128 bytes), YY-CHR Pal (a .pal for the YY-CHR editor), Gray & Rainbow (palette lines 0 to 2 become grays and a rainbow); Tiles view - Dump VRAM and Load VRAM; Sprites view - Dump (the list as text)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Debugger > CPU Debugger:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Stops the 68000 of the game. Break stops it at the next instruction; Continue (F5), Step Into (F7), Step Over (F8), Step Out and Run to Cursor (F4) let it go on. The window shows the instructions around where it stopped (disassembled; the wheel scrolls, Go jumps to an address), the registers and the calls that led there. Double-click a line (or press F9) to set a breakpoint on that instruction (a red dot); Add... also makes breakpoints for a read or a write of an address or range. The address of a read or write breakpoint (68000) is the one the game reaches with the top bits and the repeats of the work RAM left out: a breakpoint at $FF8000 also stops an access through $FFFF8000 (a short absolute address) or $E08000. Add... can also watch the Z80's memory (Read and Write; a Z80 instruction cannot be a breakpoint, as the core does not report them) and the VDP's VRAM, CRAM and VSRAM (Read and Write; a DMA from the 68000's memory into them stops the game too and the message says so; a VRAM fill or copy is not seen). A Read breakpoint on the 68000's memory also stops a DMA that reads there for the VDP. While the game is stopped the other debug windows show it as it is. Main 68000 only (a 32X game: its 68000 and both SH-2s, picked next to Go, with execute breakpoints only; the Plugins must be the same version), the Z80 only through its memory (no stepping), not during netplay."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Debugger > RAM Search:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Narrows down the addresses of the game's RAM (68000 or Z80, a 32X game also its SDRAM) by what their values do. Pick the size, the type, what to compare with (previous value, a value, an address, the number of changes) and how (less, greater, equal, different by N, modulo N), then press Search again and again as the game changes. Reset starts over, Undo takes back a step, Eliminate drops the picked results, Autosearch searches all the time, Add Watch sends the picked results to the RAM Watch."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Debugger > RAM Watch:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   A list of addresses whose values are shown while the game runs, each with a note (a value turns red for a moment when it changes). New..., Edit... (or double-click), Remove, Duplicate, Separator, Move Up and Move Down work on the list; New and Edit can also put a value into the address. New List, Open..., Save... and Append... use the .wch files of Gens. The list of a game is kept in the RAM Watch folder and comes back next time. Addresses: the 68000's (RAM E00000 to FFFFFF, Z80 RAM A00000, the ROM from 0, a 32X game's SDRAM at 6000000) or the Z80's for the 8-bit consoles (RAM C000 to FFFF)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Record Input..., Play Input Recording..., Stop Input Recording / Playback:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Record Input saves the state of the game at that moment and every button you press after it into a .gpm file (named after the game and the time, in the Input folder inside the recordings folder, like the other recordings); Play Input Recording loads that state and presses the same buttons again, exactly: for speedruns and bug reports. It must be played with the same game. Stop ends either one; closing the game does too. Not during netplay. These are in Tools > Record."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Record VGM File:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Records what the sound chips play into a .vgm file that music players supporting the format can play. Like the other recordings it goes in the recordings folder (in its VGM folder, in the console's folder), named after the game and the time, and Stop Recording (Alt+F11) ends it (Record VGM File is grayed while a recording runs). Shift+F10 starts it. A recording that starts while a game plays begins with the state of the sound chips, so the instruments are there even if the song started earlier (the FM chip is read back, whichever FM Chip is chosen, so this holds after a state was loaded too). At the end every held note is released and the PSG turned down, so the file does not end on a ringing tone. A 32X game's PWM sound is recorded too; only some players can play it (the libvgm-based ones). It is in Tools > Record, and grayed out until a game is loaded."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Netplay... / Stop Netplay:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Two-player game over a local network: one PC hosts, the other joins. Both need the same game, the same program file and the same settings. The status bar shows the ping to the other player and whether a different delay would suit it better. A lost connection is given a minute to come back. The Host box can open the router's port for you (UPnP, off until you tick it)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Record:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Video + Audio (Shift+F11) makes an .mp4, Audio Only (Ctrl+F11) a .wav. Files go in the recordings folder, each kind in a folder of its own (Video, Audio, GIF, VGM, Input) and in it a folder for the console. How a video recording looks is set in Settings > Video > Video Recording: its size (1x to 4x the console's own picture, 2x by default), its quality (automatic, or 4 to 32 Mbit/s) and its shape (the console's own, stretched to 4:3, or stretched to 16:9). Record Animated GIF makes a picture-only .gif (20 pictures a second, up to 6 seconds, then it stops by itself) with Ctrl+F12. Stop Recording (Alt+F11) ends any of these, including a Record VGM File recording."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Save Screenshot (F12):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Saves a PNG named after the game and the time."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Screenshot Output:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Corrected (the default): the picture stretched to the 4:3 shape of a TV, 640x480. Raw: the console's own picture at its own size (for example 320x224), not stretched. Final: like Corrected, but with the Render Filter applied (the same as Corrected when no filter is on). Settings > Video only (no longer in the Tools menu)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Screenshot Size:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   1x to 4x: how many times larger than the output's own size the screenshot is (Corrected 640x480, Raw the console's own size, Final the filter's size). Raw is enlarged in whole pixels so it stays sharp. Settings > Video only."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Settings...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The options of the menus, and some more, on tabs (General, Video, Audio, Picture, Input, Options and OSD). Hover over an option for a short description (it hides after 30 seconds). Restore Defaults (bottom left) opens a list: All Tabs puts every setting on all tabs back to its default, keeping your controls, game folders and cheats; This Tab does the same for the tab you are looking at only. The window grows taller when a tab needs more room. OK keeps what you changed; Cancel (also the X and Esc) puts every setting back as it was when the window opened. Changes show at once while the window is open, so you can see their effect; the buttons that open another window or act on files (Game Folders, Bezel Image, Clear Cover Cache, Restore Dialog) work immediately and are not undone by Cancel. The windows that have their own settings open from a button."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Region:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Which region the console reports to the game: Auto Detect (from the game), USA, Europe or Japan."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Preferred Region Order:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   With Region on Auto Detect: which region a Mega Drive game gets when its header names several (such as UE or JUE). The first region of the order that the game supports is taken. USA -> JPN -> EUR is the order the program has always used (the first choice of Options > Region > Preferred Order in the menu, which has the same six orders). A game whose header is wrong and that the core knows keeps the region the core gives it. Not used when a region is chosen, and in 32X games."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Force VDP Mode:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Runs the video timing at 60 Hz (NTSC) or 50 Hz (PAL), whatever the region says. None (the default) forces nothing: the timing follows the game's region."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Console:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Auto Detect picks the console from the game; or choose a specific model to force it (Options > Console, and the Console drop-down in Settings > Emulation)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Data Folders...:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Settings > General. Moves any of the folders beside the program (BIOS, Cheats, Images, Languages, Profiles, Recordings, Saves, Screenshots, Shaders, States) somewhere else. Each folder has a path box and a ... button to choose a folder. The boxes show the folders beside the program as .\\BIOS, .\\Saves and so on (relative to the program); a box that is empty, or holds that, keeps the folder there. Restore Defaults puts every folder back; OK keeps the choices, Cancel does not. Files in the old place are not moved, and the Languages folder is used from the new place after a restart. Hover over a folder for a description."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Language:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The language of the menus, windows, messages and the User Guide. System uses the language of Windows when the Languages folder has a file for it (English when not). The list is the .po files in the Languages folder (copy Template.pot to a new name and translate it; a text left empty stays English). Changing it asks to restart the program."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Lock-On Cartridge:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Attaches a Game Genie, Action Replay or Sonic & Knuckles to the game. Settings > Emulation only (not in the Options menu)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Boot from BIOS When Available:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Runs the console's start-up ROM when a BIOS file is present (see below). Settings > Emulation only (not in the Options menu)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Remove Per-Line Sprite Limit:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Takes off the limit on the sprites of a scanline: the real console draws at most 20 sprites (16 on a screen of 256 pixels) of a line on a Mega Drive, 8 on a Master System and 4 on an SG-1000, and some games flicker their sprites to get around it. With this ticked every sprite of a line is drawn, so such games stop flickering; a few games that use the limit on purpose to hide something may look wrong. Off by default. Settings > Emulation only (not in the Options menu); not used in 32X games."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Enhanced Per-Tile Vertical Scroll, Vertical Scroll Limit:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Lets each 8-pixel tile column scroll up and down on its own, instead of in 16-pixel pairs, in the few games that use that scroll mode (the limit: where two neighboring tiles differ by that many pixels or more, it is not used). Settings > Emulation only."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  System Lock-Ups:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Emulates the lock-ups the real console has when a game uses an address that does not exist. Turn off only for a few demos and homebrew. Settings > Emulation only."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  CPU Speed:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Speed of the emulated main 68000 (and the Mega CD's second one), 50% to 400%. Above 100% can reduce slowdown but can cause glitches. The sound chips are not changed; always 100% during netplay. Settings > Emulation only."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Emulate Address Error Exceptions:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Hardware-accurate crashes on bad memory access; turn off only for a few hacked games. Settings > Emulation only (not in the Options menu)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Emulate Mega CD Disc Latency:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Makes the Mega CD drive seek slowly like the real one (some games need it); off makes loading instant. Settings > Emulation only (not in the Options menu)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  CD Image Cache:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Reads a whole CHD disc image into memory when the disc is loaded, so the game never waits for the drive or the disk. Only for CHD images (they stay compressed in memory); it takes effect the next time a disc is loaded. Settings > Emulation only (not in the Options menu); not used in 32X games."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Show Extended Game Gear Screen:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Shows the whole Game Gear picture instead of only the part its screen displayed. Settings > Emulation only (not in the Options menu)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Show Master System Side Borders:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Off by default, which crops the 8-pixel columns at each side; tick to show them. Settings > Emulation only (not in the Options menu)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Show Frame Rate (Ctrl+T):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Frames per second on screen. (Settings > OSD.) Ctrl+T switches it on and off, and it can be put on a pad button in Gamepad Hotkeys."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Messages for Changed Options:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   On by default: changing an option shows its name and new value (needs Show Messages) (Settings > OSD)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Message Position:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Which corner of the picture messages appear in: top left, top right, bottom left (the default) or bottom right (Settings > OSD)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Message Time:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   1, 2, 3 (the default), 4, 5, 6 or 7 seconds: how long each message stays on screen (Settings > OSD). Messages Shown (1 to 7, default 3) sets how many of the latest messages stay together, the newest nearest the corner and older ones stacked beside it; each fades out when its time is up."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Font Color:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   White (the default), Yellow, Green, Cyan, Orange, Pink, Red or Black: the color of messages, the frame rate and the pause menu title (Settings > OSD). The outline turns light for dark colors, so the text stays readable on any picture."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Font Size:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Small, Normal, Large or Extra Large: the size of the text of messages, the frame rate and the pause menu (Settings > OSD)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Frame Rate Position:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Which corner of the picture the frame rate appears in: top left, top right (the default), bottom left or bottom right (Settings > OSD)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Backup State:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Off, or every 1, 2, 5 or 10 minutes while a game runs: a state is saved quietly into three rolling backups. Restore one with Load State > Load Backup State. Not used during netplay (Settings > Emulation)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Run-Ahead (Emulation menu, or Settings > Emulation, Input Lag; hotkey Ctrl+E):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Off by default: Off, 1 Frame or 2 Frames. The hotkey (Input > Hotkeys: Run-Ahead) steps through the three while a game runs and shows the new setting. Cuts the delay between a button press and the picture. Each frame the game is run 1 or 2 frames ahead in secret with the same buttons, the picture of the last one is shown, and the game goes back (a saved state) to where it really is; the sound is the sound a normal run plays, one frame earlier (the sound chain is kept across the loads). A game that reacts 1 or 2 frames later than the console did feels like the console; a game with a long built-in delay needs 2. It makes the PC work 2 or 3 times as hard for each frame, so use it with room to spare (a Mega CD or 32X game, whose saved state is bigger and which is heavier to run, costs the most). It is not used in netplay, while recording a video, audio or GIF, logging VGM or making or playing an input recording, while Skip Intro runs, or at a Game Speed other than 100%. A rare game can glitch with it. It is done by the program, the emulator core is not changed."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Skip Intro on Launch:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Off, or 5, 10, 15, 20, 25 or 30 seconds: when a game starts, its first seconds of game time run at full speed to get past logos and intros; this takes much less real time than the seconds chosen. A press of a game button ends the skip early. Not used when resuming a game or during netplay (Settings > Emulation). Off by default."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Enable Log:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Writes genesis-plus-ex.log in the Logs folder next to the program: the program file (its size, date and SHA-256, to tell an official build from another), the machine (Windows, processor, memory, screens and sound devices) and every line of the settings file at the start; every command chosen (with its menu), every window opened and closed, which window has the focus, every change of the window's size, and every setting that changes; for each game its file, size and checksum, the ROM header, how the console was set up (region, CD hardware, cartridge hardware), the picture and sound it runs with and, when it closes, how it went (time, frames, slowest frame); resets, saved and loaded states, the cheat codes read and when the ROM ones go in, messages, errors, netplay events and crashes. Off by default; send it along with a bug report (Settings > Emulation)."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Keep a Cover Cache:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   On by default. Each finished cover is saved (small and compressed) in one file, Images\\Covers\\thumbs.dat, so the next time the program starts the grid covers appear almost at once instead of being read and scaled again. A cover you change, or a new cover size or frame, is made again by itself; the first run, and a new size, still take the usual time. The same option keeps what the game list found out about each file (the console of a disc, an archive or a .chd) in Covers\\library.dat, so the list loads faster the next time. The Refresh button of the game list also deletes that file and looks at every file again. Clear Cover Cache (next to it in Settings > General) deletes the saved covers and that file. The file holds roughly 20 to 60 KB per game and size, and is emptied by itself when it passes 300 MB."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Enable Crash Report:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   If the program crashes it writes genesis-plus-ex-crash.txt and genesis-plus-ex-crash.dmp in the Logs folder next to the program, whether or not Enable Log is on (only the last crash is kept). Off by default (Settings > Emulation). Send both with a bug report."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Show Messages:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Short messages over the picture, such as \"Saved to slot 3\" or the name of a screenshot. Changing an option, from a menu or from Settings, shows its name and new value too (\"Smooth Scaling: On\", \"Scanlines: 50%\"). Untick to hide them (the status bar still shows them). (Settings > OSD.)"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "HELP"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  Keyboard Shortcuts (F1):"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   The list of hotkeys."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  User Guide:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   This page. Pick a section on the left to show just that part. The window can be made bigger or maximized."
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "  About Genesis Plus EX:"
msgstr ""

#: dialogs.c:4213
msgid "   Version and credits."
msgstr ""

